 ## Background
 The concept of extending NeRF with feature branches originated from the need to move beyond simple color reconstruction to include semantic and geometric information [S1]. The Distilled Feature Field (DFF) method, introduced by [S1], was among the first to distill 2D teacher network features into a 3D feature field for zero-shot segmentation. Subsequent works expanded this to include specific applications such as pose estimation (PI-NeRF [S8]), object segmentation (Object Segmentation Component [S2]), and scene manipulation (Control-NeRF [S3]). The family has evolved to include complex architectures like GP-NeRF [S6] for context-aware perception and SCNeRF [S7] for sparse-view settings.
